# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE FIRST PINNED WAS A LIST NOBODY REVIEWED. Six names — `fmt`,
# `test / ubuntu-latest`, `test / macos-latest`, `test / windows-latest`,
# `test (release)` and `qemu-validation` — lived only in the repository
# settings page, typed in by hand, with no diff, no author and no reviewer.
# They were correct on the day: the audit in antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197
# checked, and the check-runs on main's HEAD and on the newest pull_request
# check-suite were exactly those six, all success. The sibling rust-img-qcow2
# was already in the state that follows from not checking, producing a
# `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu` check that was never required by anyone.
#
# ONE NAME NOW, BECAUSE A JOB STANDS FOR THE REST (#111). Moving the list into
# this file made it reviewable; it did not stop it drifting, because the drift
# is a diff nobody is obliged to write. Both directions were one ordinary edit
# away:
#
#   * add a leg to ci.yml's `os:` matrix and it reports on every pull request,
#     requiring nothing and gating nothing — the rust-img-qcow2 state again.
#   * rename a job, or mis-spell one here, and the declared name is a name no
#     job produces. GitHub reads a required-and-absent context as PENDING, not
#     as failed, so with enforce_admins on nothing merges and there is no
#     failure to point at. The two spellings in ci.yml are what makes that easy
#     to do by hand: a matrix leg is "test / ubuntu-latest", with spaces around
#     the slash, because Actions joins the job's `name:` to the matrix value
#     that way, while `test (release)` is a job whose own `name:` carries
#     literal parentheses and no slash. Neither is a convention, so neither can
#     be inferred from the other, and `test / release` is a name that never
#     reports.
#
# So ci.yml grew one job, `ci-ok`, whose `needs:` names every other job in the
# file and which fails unless each concluded `success` — failure, cancellation
# and skipping all counting against it, with `if: always()` so a skipped
# dependency is judged rather than leaving the aggregate skipped beside it.
# That list lives next to the jobs it names, changed in the same diff that
# renames, splits or adds one, and the name protection asks for does not move
# when the jobs do.
#
# WHAT ci-ok STANDS FOR. All four jobs run on `pull_request` (ci.yml triggers
# on `push` to main and on `pull_request` with no branch filter), so all four
# can gate one:
#
#   test            three matrix legs, ubuntu/macos/windows-latest: build,
#                   `cargo test --locked --all-targets` under a measured output
#                   budget and a test-count floor, then clippy. Debug profile,
#                   so this is the run carrying the EXPECT_OVERFLOW_CHECKS
#                   handshake tests/ci_profile.rs insists on.
#   test-release    `test (release)`. Not a second copy of the matrix: release
#                   lets an integer overflow wrap where debug panics, so a test
#                   guarding a wrapping computation — grain tables, extent
#                   offsets, sector counts scaled by 512 — passes the debug run
#                   for the wrong reason. It is the only run that compiles the
#                   profile that is published.
#   qemu-validation cross-validates our reader and writer against `qemu-img`,
#                   the only check that compares this crate to an
#                   implementation we did not write.
#   fmt             `cargo fmt --check`.
#
# ONLY ci.yml CAN GATE A MERGE, and this repository makes that easy to get
# wrong: release.yml runs jobs named `test` and `qemu-validation` too — the
# same names — but it triggers on a `v*.*.*` tag, which happens AFTER the
# merge it would have gated, and fuzz.yml is workflow_dispatch plus a nightly
# cron. A required check that only a tag or a schedule can produce never
# reports on a pull request and blocks the repository permanently, which is the
# same pending-forever failure as a mis-spelled name. `needs:` cannot reach
# those jobs in any case: it names jobs in its own workflow only, which is why
# the name collision is harmless here and was worth writing down.
#
# Third-party reviewers are not in the list and do not need to be excluded by
# hand: github-protect-main.sh filters `select(.app.slug=="github-actions")`
# on every one of its queries, so a review app cannot become required.
#
# The name must match the check-run name GitHub reports, which for this job is
# both its key and its `name:` — `ci-ok`.
#
# tests/ci_aggregate_gate.rs holds both halves to each other: every job in
# ci.yml has to appear in `ci-ok`'s `needs`, and this file has to require
# `ci-ok` and nothing else.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
